1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,424
Why do everyday investors cash out

2
00:00:02,424 --> 00:00:04,920
winning stocks for tiny gains while

3
00:00:04,920 --> 00:00:07,558
clinging to sinking losers for years?

4
00:00:07,558 --> 00:00:08,770
Pride and regret.

5
00:00:08,770 --> 00:00:11,165
Hersh Shefrin and Meir Statman named

6
00:00:11,165 --> 00:00:13,560
this the Disposition Effect in 1985.

7
00:00:13,560 --> 00:00:14,092
In 1998,

8
00:00:14,092 --> 00:00:17,152
Terrance Odean analyzed ten thousand brokerage

9
00:00:17,152 --> 00:00:19,481
accounts and found the sold winners

10
00:00:19,481 --> 00:00:21,277
went on to beat held losers

11
00:00:21,277 --> 00:00:23,140
by three-point-four percent.

12
00:00:23,140 --> 00:00:25,857
Selling a winner locks in quick pride,

13
00:00:25,857 --> 00:00:28,573
while holding a loser delays admitting

14
00:00:28,573 --> 00:00:29,288
a mistake,

15
00:00:29,288 --> 00:00:31,934
leaving you trimming flowers to water

16
00:00:31,934 --> 00:00:32,720
your weeds.

17
00:00:32,720 --> 00:00:34,889
Erase ego from your portfolio:

18
00:00:34,889 --> 00:00:37,492
automate low-cost broad index funds,

19
00:00:37,492 --> 00:00:39,517
or ask whether you would buy

20
00:00:39,517 --> 00:00:41,758
a losing stock today with fresh

21
00:00:41,758 --> 00:00:42,120
cash.

22
00:00:42,120 --> 00:00:44,541
Judge every holding by tomorrow's math

23
00:00:44,541 --> 00:00:46,962
instead of yesterday's purchase price,

24
00:00:46,962 --> 00:00:48,810
and let true compounders run.
